          "Now, turn to the specific institutional division of labor behind the multilateral instinct just described. Article 65 of the Grundgesetz gives the Chancellor Richtlinienkompetenz, the authority to set the general guidelines of policy, but within those guidelines each minister runs their own portfolio under the Ressortprinzip, departmental autonomy, and the Auswärtiges Amt, the Foreign Ministry, retains genuine day-to-day authority over diplomatic practice, embassy instructions, and much of foreign-policy implementation. Under Germany's coalition norms the Foreign Ministry is traditionally, though not universally, allocated to a junior coalition partner as part of the Koalitionsvertrag's distribution of cabinet posts, which means the minister actually running German diplomacy day to day is frequently from a different party than the Chancellor, with a genuinely separate political base and a separate set of instincts about what German foreign policy should prioritize. The mechanism that keeps this workable in practice is continuous coordination at the Staatssekretär level and through the Bundessicherheitsrat, covered later, where Chancellery and Foreign Ministry officials meet regularly, but no formal veto forces the two offices into agreement, and Richtlinienkompetenz itself has rarely been tested as a hard legal instrument precisely because chancellors prefer negotiation over confrontation with their own coalition partners.",
