          "Now, turn to the Bundestag's own direct constitutional check on this executive machinery. German Bundeswehr deployments abroad generally require prior Bundestag authorization under Parlamentsvorbehalt, a principle the Bundesverfassungsgericht derived rather than one written explicitly into the Grundgesetz's text, established by the court's 1994 AWACS/Somalia ruling, BVerfGE 90, 286, which held that any deployment of armed German soldiers into actual or expected armed conflict requires prior, specific parliamentary consent [1]. The mechanism was subsequently codified in statutory form by the 2005 Parlamentsbeteiligungsgesetz, the Parliamentary Participation Act, which sets out the procedure, a government motion specifying mandate, mission, troop numbers, and duration, that the Bundestag must approve, and, for most missions, periodically renew, before deployment and at intervals thereafter. A narrow exception exists for Gefahr im Verzug, imminent danger, allowing rapid deployment with subsequent parliamentary approval, and routine participation in NATO's integrated command structure or purely logistical, non-combat support generally falls outside the requirement altogether, a genuinely contested line in individual cases the Bundesverfassungsgericht has had to revisit more than once as the Bundeswehr's own missions have grown more varied and less clearly bounded than a classic peacekeeping deployment, from maritime patrols to cyber-defense support to training missions that sit somewhere between combat exposure and pure instruction.",
