    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: which level of government, which coalition partner, and which formally consulted interest group actually has to sign off before this policy idea becomes real. Kooperativer Föderalismus and the Föderalismusreformen of 2006 and 2009 together set the underlying division of authority between Bund and Länder that every other concept in this lecture operates within, a division renegotiated periodically rather than fixed since 1949. Koalitionsregierung shapes what is politically achievable at the federal level in the first place, since Germany's proportional system makes single-party government nearly unheard of, sozialpartnerschaft and the Verbändeanhörung requirement bring organized interests into that process formally rather than informally, embedding consultation into the drafting stage itself, and the Vermittlungsausschuss and Schuldenbremse are the two procedural and fiscal checkpoints that can still stall or constrain a policy even after coalition partners and consulted Verbände have all signed off, one a political veto point and the other a constitutional one. Subsidiarität is the underlying philosophy explaining why so much of this stays decentralized rather than being centralized federally or at the EU level, the Sachverständigenrat is the expert voice shaping economic-policy debate before any of this formally begins, publishing its independent assessment months before a budget is even drafted, and the Bürgergeld reform shows all nine earlier ideas actually operating together on one real, traceable piece of major German legislation, from campaign commitment through Vermittlungsausschuss compromise to ongoing post-passage political contestation. As you follow any real German domestic policy fight from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and ask at each stage which actor still has to say yes, the coalition partner, the consulted Verband, the Bundesrat, the Bundesverfassungsgericht, before the policy you are watching actually becomes law.",
