        title: "Negotiating multi-party (Ampel/Jamaika-style) coalitions",
        explanation:
          "Now, turn to how the entire negotiating apparatus covered so far scales once more than two parties are actually involved. As smaller parties have gained vote share, German coalitions increasingly involve three parties rather than the traditional two, nicknamed by color combinations, Ampel, traffic light, for a red-yellow-green coalition, or Jamaika for the Jamaican flag's black-yellow-green, requiring genuinely multilateral negotiation among three sets of priorities and red lines simultaneously, not sequential two-party bargaining. The mechanism behind the added difficulty is a familiar one in the comparative coalition literature: as Wolfgang Müller and Kaare Strøm's influential comparative study of European coalition governments documents, adding even one additional veto player to a governing coalition multiplies the number of pairwise relationships that must all hold simultaneously, and a compromise stable between any two of the three parties can still be rejected outright by the third, a structural fragility with no real two-party equivalent [2]. A further mechanism worth naming is that a three-party coalition also has three separate party congresses, membership bases, and internal factions whose approval any renegotiated compromise must eventually satisfy, not just three sets of leaders in a room, which means the visible Koalitionsausschuss negotiation is often only the final stage of a much longer internal-party consultation process each partner has to run in parallel.",
